          <tr align="left" valign="top"> 
            <td height="214"> 
              <pre>PMWATCH - March 27, 2002 -- A long standing practice by AP, Reuters,
and other news outlets, is to lead their stories by allegations and
accusations made by the Israelis, even when Palestinians deny or accuse
back just as vehemently.  Indeed, when was the last time you read an AP 
story that opened with the Palestinian version of a contested version of 
reality?  

Here is blatant example of this practice: the opening paragraph of the story 
below reads:  "Two observers from an international force in Hebron were shot 
and killed when Palestinians opened fire on their car in the West Bank on Tuesday, 
the Israeli military said".

The opening paragraph could have easily read: "Two observers 
from an international force in Hebron were shot and killed when Israeli 
soldiers opened fire on their car in the West Bank on Tuesday, the 
Palestinian Authority said".

And yet, the lead paragraph has to open with with Israel allegations, 
presented basically as a fact throughout the story.  We have to wait 
until paragraph 5 to read: "Although the Israeli military said Palestinian 
gunmen were to blame, Turkish Ambassador Ahmet Uzumcu said 'it is still 
not clear who opened fire.'" 

Worse yet, the categorical denial by the Palestinian Authority is totally 
left unmentioned.  In a BBC story that same day, we read: "The Palestinian 
leadership, however, said in a statement that the Israeli army had opened fire 
on the car. 'The Israeli army bears full responsibility for this crime,' it 
said."   <a href="http://news.bbc.co.uk/hi/english/world/middle_east/newsid_1895000/1895463.stm" class="dead" aria-disabled="true">  http://news.bbc.co.uk/hi/english/world/middle_east/newsid_1895000/1895463.stm</a>

Not a word of this from the AP story. 

Please impress upon AP and all news oulets, your paper and other papers, to take a 
step back from blatantly adopting the official Israeli spin.  Unless the journalist 
was there and saw who did what, it should be a rule that if both sides deny that they 
are responsible for something, then the opening paragraph MUST reflect this -- PLUS the 
body of the story should ALWAYS contain denials by the PA when they articulate them.

Here are some previous relevant findings by PMWatch:

<a href="/sites/pmwatch.org/pmw/reports/newssummaryjuly2001.html#_Toc521668910">
http://www.pmwatch.org/pmw/reports/newssummaryJuly2001.html#_Toc521668910</a>

<a href="/sites/pmwatch.org/pmw/reports/pi/newssummaryjune2001.html#_Toc516248049">http://www.pmwatch.org/pmw/reports/pi/newssummaryjune2001.html#_Toc516248049</a>

<a href="/sites/pmwatch.org/pmw/reports/pi/newssummarymay2001.html#_Toc516248049">http://www.pmwatch.org/pmw/reports/pi/newssummarymay2001.html#_Toc516248049</a>

